# Ad-hoc `go build ./...` output. Every Makefile target passes -o bin/, which
# the rule above already covers; these appear only when someone builds a single
# main package by hand, because go then writes the binary into the working
# directory named after that package's own directory.
#
# Enumerated rather than pattern-matched because a compiled binary has no
# extension to match on, and unlike a list chasing an upstream vocabulary this
# population is bounded and regenerable — it is the module's main packages:
#
#     go list -f '{{if eq .Name "main"}}{{.ImportPath}}{{end}}' ./...
#
# ./cmd is deliberately absent. Its binary would be named "cmd", which collides
# with the source directory of the same name, and go refuses rather than
# overwriting it:
#
#     go: build output "cmd" already exists and is a directory
#
# So that file cannot appear — while the rule to ignore it WOULD match every new
# untracked file under operators/cmd/, silently swallowing a newly added source
# file in the operator's own main package. An ignore rule that can never catch
# its target and can catch the source tree is worse than no rule.
#
# Stakes are low either way: the org rule is never `git add -A`, so these are
# only reachable by naming a binary explicitly. That rule is what keeps this a
# tidiness fix rather than a safety one.
operators/agentctl
operators/metrics-shim
operators/coverage-check

# OpenTofu / Terraform
.terraform/
# .terraform.lock.hcl: track the canonical per-component locks (reproducible,
# hash-pinned provider selections across the CI + dev platforms); ignore any
# others (module/.terraform caches).
.terraform.lock.hcl
!/terraform/components/*/.terraform.lock.hcl
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terragrunt-cache/

